(5208, 669, '_home-thumb', 'field_5891155fd8204'),
(5209, 669, 'home-excerpt', 'Global mergers and acquisitions (M&A) activity surged in January, posting its best first month of the year since 2000, data compiled by Bloomberg reveals. January saw some major transactions...'),
(5210, 669, '_home-excerpt', 'field_5891162dfcc38'),
(5211, 669, 'news-post-header', 'Global mergers and acquisitions (M&A) activity surged in January, posting its best first month of the year since 2000, data compiled by Bloomberg reveals. '),
(5212, 669, '_news-post-header', 'field_5891171af8567'),
(5213, 669, 'news-post-source', 'http://www.investopedia.com/news/how-long-will-ma-surge-last-lux-atln/'),
(5214, 669, '_news-post-source', 'field_589ba71212b6e'),
(5215, 669, 'news-post-content', 'January saw some major transactions as large firms such as Luxottica Group SpA (LUX) and biotech company Actelion Ltd. (ATLN) agreed to multibillion-dollar takeovers. However, some wonder how long this will last, given President Donald Trump’s opposition to the merger between AT&amp;T Inc. (T) and Time Warner Inc. (TWX). Eyewear maker Luxottica announced that it would merge with Essilor International SA (EI) in a $49 billion transaction, according to The New York Times. Actelion, the largest biotech firm in Europe, revealed that it agreed to be sold to Johnson &amp; Johnson (JNJ), MarketWatch reported.\r\n\r\nThese deals helped total global M&amp;A activity reach $224 billion in January, according to data compiled by Bloomberg. (See also: Mergers and Acquisitions: Introduction.)\r\n\r\n<strong>M&amp;A Drivers</strong>\r\n\r\nIn 2015, many companies held off deals due to volatile market conditions and rising bond yields, which make M&amp;A financing more expensive. That created pent-up demand that exploded in January, according to analysts who spoke with Bloomberg. However, other key variables are helping fuel strong M&amp;A activity, according to Joe Culley, head of equities &amp; investment banking at Janney Montgomery Scott LLC., in an interview with Investopedia. “In general, M&amp;A is being driven by stable credit markets, equity valuations that are either at or near all-time highs and also pedestrian GDP growth in many parts of the world,” he told Investopedia. Corporations and private equity firms are are also sitting on record levels of cash, Culley said. (See also: Where do companies keep their cash?)\r\n\r\n<strong>The Trump Effect</strong>\r\n\r\nSome market observers say Trump’s objection to the AT&amp;T-Time Warner deal is a reason for concern, according to Bloomberg. As a result, some analysts say it’s likely that many chose to do deals in January before Trump “really gets into a stride,” Jasper Lawler, who works for London Capital Group as a market analyst, told Bloomberg. Not everyone shares this concern. Trump has “assembled a fairly pro-business cabinet,” noted Culley.\r\n\r\nConsultant Paul Aversano told Investopedia that President Trump has expressed an interest in cutting regulations and firing up economic growth, which could both support M&amp;A activity.Aversano is global practice leader for consultancy Alvarez &amp; Marsal’s Transaction Advisory Group. In particular, cutting regulation could “help drive M&amp;A,” Aversano said.\r\n\r\nBy: Charles Bovaird'),
